{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of light that only exists at 6 a.m. in late summer. It isn't the harsh, demanding light of noon, or the nostalgic, golden light of sunset. It's a thin, blue-tinted, tentative light, as if the sun itself is still deciding whether to get out of bed. I love that light.\n\nI love it because it belongs to no one. The world hasn't fully woken up yet. The streets are empty except for delivery trucks and a few runners who look determined to outpace their thoughts. The air is cooler than it has any right to be, smelling faintly of cut grass and distant coffee. In that hour, your apartment feels like a spacecraft, quiet and humming, detached from the busy planet below.\n\nIn that hour, you can be anyone. You can sit with a chipped mug and read a book you've read three times before, and it will feel new. You can write badly and not feel embarrassed, because no one is watching, not even the critical voice in your own head, which is still half-asleep.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives performing for an audience, real or imagined. We curate our words, our clothes, our opinions. But the early morning asks for nothing. It doesn't need you to be productive or profound. It just lets you exist.\n\nMaybe that's why I chase it. Not for discipline or wellness points, but for permission. Permission to simply be, before the day asks me to become.",
